Determine which of the following are multiplicative:

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{(a)} $f(n) = 1$ for all $n \geq 1$.

Check: $f(mn) = 1 = 1 \cdot 1 = f(m) \cdot f(n)$ for all $m, n$. \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

Multiplicative (in fact, completely multiplicative).

\textbf{(b)} $f(n) = n$ (the identity function).

Check: $f(mn) = mn = f(m) \cdot f(n)$ for all $m, n$. \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

Completely multiplicative.

\textbf{(c)} $f(n) = n^2$.

Check: $f(mn) = (mn)^2 = m^2 n^2 = f(m) \cdot f(n)$. \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

Completely multiplicative.

\textbf{(d)} $f(n) = n + 1$.

Check with $m = 2, n = 3$ (coprime):
\[
f(6) = 7, \quad f(2) \cdot f(3) = 3 \cdot 4 = 12 \neq 7 \quad \textcolor{red}{\times}
\]

\textbf{Not} multiplicative.

\textbf{(e)} $f(n) = 0$ for all $n$.

By definition, an identically-zero function is excluded. \textcolor{red}{$\times$}

\textbf{Not} multiplicative (fails the non-zero condition).

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faCheckCircle\ \textbf{Summary: Arithmetic Functions}}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|p{3.5cm}|p{5cm}|p{4cm}|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Type} & \textbf{Condition (when $\gcd(m,n)=1$)} & \textbf{Value at 1} \\
\hline
Multiplicative & $f(mn) = f(m) \cdot f(n)$ & $f(1) = 1$ \\
\hline
Additive & $f(mn) = f(m) + f(n)$ & $f(1) = 0$ \\
\hline
Completely Multiplicative & $f(mn) = f(m) \cdot f(n)$ for \textbf{all} $m,n$ & $f(1) = 1$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

