Find the remainder when $5^{38}$ is divided by 11.

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Check conditions.

$11$ is prime and $\gcd(5,11) = 1$. Apply FLT: $5^{10} \equiv 1 \pmod{11}$.

\textbf{Step 2:} Write exponent in terms of 10.
\[
38 = 10 \times 3 + 8
\]

\textbf{Step 3:} Simplify.
\begin{align*}
5^{38} &= 5^{10 \times 3 + 8} = \left(5^{10}\right)^3 \cdot 5^8\\
       &\equiv 1^3 \cdot 5^8 \pmod{11}\\
       &\equiv 5^8 \pmod{11}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 4:} Compute $5^8 \pmod{11}$ by successive squaring.
\begin{align*}
5^1 &\equiv 5 \pmod{11}\\
5^2 &\equiv 25 \equiv 3 \pmod{11}\\
5^4 &\equiv 3^2 = 9 \pmod{11}\\
5^8 &\equiv 9^2 = 81 \equiv 81 - 77 = 4 \pmod{11}
\end{align*}

\[
5^{38} \equiv 4 \pmod{11}
\]

\textbf{Solution:} $\boxed{5^{38} \equiv 4 \pmod{11}}$

\textbf{Verification:} $5^{10} \equiv 1$ so $5^{40} \equiv 1$, giving
$5^{38} \equiv 5^{-2} \equiv (5^2)^{-1} \equiv 25^{-1} \equiv 3^{-1} \pmod{11}$.

Since $3 \times 4 = 12 \equiv 1 \pmod{11}$, we get $3^{-1} \equiv 4$. \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

