\textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{Proof:}}

\textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{Step 1: Show $p \mid (a^{pq} - a)$.}}

From hypothesis: $a^q \equiv a \pmod{p}$.

Raise both sides to the power $p$:
\[
\left(a^q\right)^p \equiv a^p \pmod{p}
\]
\[
a^{pq} \equiv a^p \equiv a \pmod{p}
\]

(The last step uses $a^p \equiv a \pmod p$ from FLT applied to prime $p$.)

So $p \mid (a^{pq} - a)$.

\textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{Step 2: Show $q \mid (a^{pq} - a)$.}}

By symmetry (using $a^p \equiv a \pmod{q}$ from hypothesis):
\[
a^{pq} = \left(a^p\right)^q \equiv a^q \equiv a \pmod{q}
\]

So $q \mid (a^{pq} - a)$.

\textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{Step 3: Combine.}}

Since $p$ and $q$ are distinct primes, $\gcd(p,q) = 1$. So:
\[
p \mid (a^{pq}-a) \text{ and } q \mid (a^{pq}-a) \implies pq \mid (a^{pq}-a)
\]
\[
a^{pq} \equiv a \pmod{pq}
\]

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Conclusion:}} $a^{pq} \equiv a \pmod{pq}$. \qed

