For any non-negative integer $n$, prove $51 \mid (10^{32n+9} - 7)$.

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

Note $51 = 3 \times 17$. We show $3 \mid (10^{32n+9} - 7)$ and
$17 \mid (10^{32n+9} - 7)$ separately.

\textbf{Part 1: Modulo 3.}

$10 \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$, so $10^{32n+9} \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$.

$7 \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$.

$10^{32n+9} - 7 \equiv 1 - 1 = 0 \pmod{3}$. \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

\textbf{Part 2: Modulo 17.}

$\gcd(10, 17) = 1$. By FLT: $10^{16} \equiv 1 \pmod{17}$.

Write $32n + 9 = 16(2n) + 9$:
\[
10^{32n+9} = (10^{16})^{2n} \cdot 10^9 \equiv 1 \cdot 10^9 = 10^9 \pmod{17}
\]

Compute $10^9 \pmod{17}$:
\begin{align*}
10^1 &\equiv 10 \pmod{17}\\
10^2 &\equiv 100 = 5 \times 17 + 15 \equiv 15 \equiv -2 \pmod{17}\\
10^4 &\equiv (-2)^2 = 4 \pmod{17}\\
10^8 &\equiv 4^2 = 16 \equiv -1 \pmod{17}\\
10^9 &\equiv -1 \times 10 = -10 \equiv 7 \pmod{17}
\end{align*}

So $10^{32n+9} \equiv 7 \pmod{17}$, giving:
\[
10^{32n+9} - 7 \equiv 7 - 7 = 0 \pmod{17} \quad \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}
\]

\textbf{Combine:} $3 \mid$ and $17 \mid$, with $\gcd(3,17)=1$:
\[
51 \mid (10^{32n+9} - 7) \quad \textcolor{green}{\checkmark} \qed
\]

