Verify Wilson's Theorem for $p = 5, 7$.

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{(a)} $p = 5$:
\[
(5-1)! = 4! = 24 = 5 \times 4 + 4 \equiv 4 \equiv -1 \pmod{5} \quad \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}
\]

\textbf{(b)} $p = 7$:
\[
(7-1)! = 6! = 720 = 7 \times 102 + 6 \equiv 6 \equiv -1 \pmod{7} \quad \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}
\]

The \textbf{Möbius function} $\mu : \mathbb{Z}^+ \to \{-1, 0, 1\}$ is defined by:
\[
\mu(n) =
\begin{cases}
1 & \text{if } n = 1\\
(-1)^k & \text{if } n = p_1 p_2 \cdots p_k \text{ (product of } k \text{ distinct primes)}\\
0 & \text{if } p^2 \mid n \text{ for some prime } p \text{ (}n \text{ has a squared factor)}
\end{cases}
\]

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Key Points:}}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item $\mu(n) = 0$ whenever $n$ has a repeated prime factor (``square-full'')
    \item $\mu(n) = \pm 1$ when $n$ is \textbf{squarefree} (no repeated prime factor)
    \item $\mu$ is multiplicative: $\mu(mn) = \mu(m)\mu(n)$ when $\gcd(m,n)=1$
    \item $\mu(1) = 1$, $\mu(p) = -1$ for any prime $p$, $\mu(p^2) = 0$
\end{itemize}

Given $F(n) = n$ (so $F(n) = \sum_{d|n}\phi(d)$ by Gauss), recover $f = \phi$ using
the inversion formula.

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

By Möbius inversion with $F(n) = n$:
\[
f(n) = \sum_{d \mid n} \mu(d)\, F\!\left(\frac{n}{d}\right) = \sum_{d \mid n} \mu(d) \cdot \frac{n}{d}
\]

\textbf{Verify for $n = 6$:} Divisors of 6: $1, 2, 3, 6$.
\begin{align*}
f(6) &= \mu(1)\cdot 6 + \mu(2)\cdot 3 + \mu(3)\cdot 2 + \mu(6)\cdot 1\\
     &= 1(6) + (-1)(3) + (-1)(2) + (1)(1)\\
     &= 6 - 3 - 2 + 1 = 2
\end{align*}

Indeed $\phi(6) = 2$. \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

\textbf{Verify for $n = 12$:} Divisors: $1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12$.
\begin{align*}
f(12) &= \mu(1)(12)+\mu(2)(6)+\mu(3)(4)+\mu(4)(3)+\mu(6)(2)+\mu(12)(1)\\
      &= 12 + (-1)(6) + (-1)(4) + 0 + (1)(2) + 0\\
      &= 12 - 6 - 4 + 2 = 4
\end{align*}

Indeed $\phi(12) = 4$. \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

This confirms $\phi(n) = \displaystyle\sum_{d \mid n}\mu(d)\cdot\dfrac{n}{d}$.

\textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{Forward Direction ($\Leftarrow$):}}

If $n = p$ is prime, then every integer in $\{1, 2, \ldots, p-1\}$ is coprime to $p$
(since $p$ has no divisors other than 1 and itself). So $\phi(p) = p-1$. \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

\textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{Backward Direction ($\Rightarrow$):}}

Suppose $\phi(n) = n-1$. We show $n$ must be prime.

Assume $n$ is composite. Then $n$ has a divisor $d$ with $1 < d < n$, so
$\gcd(d, n) = d > 1$, meaning $d$ is \textbf{not} counted by $\phi(n)$.

The integers counted by $\phi(n)$ are from $\{1, \ldots, n-1\}$, which has $n-1$
elements. For $\phi(n) = n-1$, every element of $\{1, \ldots, n-1\}$ must be coprime
to $n$.

But $d \in \{1, \ldots, n-1\}$ with $\gcd(d,n) > 1$ — contradiction.

Hence $n$ cannot be composite, so $n$ is prime. \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Conclusion:}} $\phi(n) = n-1$ if and only if $n$ is prime. \qed
